{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III inherited the county at a young age and was under the regency of his mother until he came of age. His early rule was marked by political instability and external threats from Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. Despite his youth Raymond III showed early signs of leadership and began to assert his authority over the county. He was known for his ambition and desire to expand his influence in the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became one of the most powerful nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem due to his marriage to Eschiva of Bures which gave him claims to the lordship of Galilee. He was also involved in the politics of the Crusader states and often acted as a mediator between the king and other barons. His relationship with King Baldwin IV was complicated. Baldwin IV suffered from leprosy and Raymond III was named regent during the king’s early illness. However their alliance soured over time due to disagreements on military strategy and succession. Raymond III opposed the rise of Guy of Lusignan as heir and supported the claims of Sibylla’s daughter Isabella. This created deep divisions within the nobility and weakened the kingdom’s unity.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who advocated for diplomacy with Saladin rather than direct confrontation. He even signed a truce with the Muslim leader which angered many of his fellow crusaders. When the truce was broken Raymond III joined the crusader army but was not fully trusted by his peers. He was present at the disastrous battle and survived the defeat. After Hattin he retreated to Tripoli and tried to organize a defense against Saladin’s advancing forces. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of an era of strong local leadership in the Crusader states. Raymond III left behind a legacy of political maneuvering and a divided kingdom that could not withstand the rising power of the Muslim world.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, 